{#
	Site search field.

	Used in two places that need it for different reasons: the search results
	page, where the heading already says what the field is for, and the 404,
	where it is the only recovery path a visitor has that isn't "go home".

	`hide_label` covers exactly that difference. The label is always a real
	<label for> either way — never a placeholder standing in for one, because a
	placeholder disappears the moment someone starts typing, and hiding it
	visually is not the same as not having one.

	`show_button` is the 404's own. Figma `error-search` (22:152) draws a bare
	rounded field with no submit control beside it, so the button is dropped
	there rather than invented. Enter still submits: a form whose only field is
	a text input has implicit submission by spec, with or without a button, and
	`type="search"` gives a touch keyboard its own submit key. On the results
	page, where submitting is the page's main action, the button stays.

	`variant` keeps the button hierarchy honest where there is one: primary on
	the search page, secondary anywhere "back to home" already owns primary.

	Expects: action, value, label, hide_label, id, variant, show_button,
	placeholder.
#}

{% set field_id = id|default('search-field') %}
{% set show_button = show_button is defined ? show_button : true %}

<form class="search-form" role="search" method="get" action="{{ action }}">

	<label
		class="search-form__label{{ hide_label ? ' search-form__label--hidden' : '' }}"
		for="{{ field_id }}"
	>{{ label|default('חיפוש באתר') }}</label>

	<div class="search-form__row">
		<input
			class="form-field__control search-form__control"
			id="{{ field_id }}"
			type="search"
			name="s"
			value="{{ value }}"
			placeholder="{{ placeholder|default('מה אתם מחפשים?') }}"
		>
		{% if show_button %}
			<button class="button button--{{ variant|default('primary') }}" type="submit">חיפוש</button>
		{% endif %}
	</div>

</form>
